      "claim": "ThH has very low oral bioavailability, but it is a phenolic morphinan with a cyclopropylmethyl group and thiophene; its poor F is attributed to phenol conjugation and high first-pass metabolism, whereas the query has a methyl ether and no free phenol.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C26"],
      "claim": "M6G has poor oral bioavailability, but it is a polar morphine glucuronide/acetylated derivative with poor membrane permeability; the query is a smaller, more lipophilic tertiary-amine alkaloid without this polar conjugate liability.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C30"],
      "claim": "Terameprocol has very low oral bioavailability (max 2.5%), but it is a highly lipophilic lignan with poor aqueous solubility and no ionizable center; the query has an ionizable tertiary amine and lower logP, giving a different absorption profile.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "Amisulpride, a basic amine-containing drug-like molecule, achieves approximately 50% absolute oral bioavailability in healthy volunteers, demonstrating that basic amine scaffolds can reach F >= 20%; transferability is low due to different scaffold, but the direction supports high.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C28"],
      "claim": "Morphine, a rigid alkaloid with a tertiary amine and ether bridge, shows absolute oral bioavailability of 23.8% despite having two free hydroxyls; the query is more lipophilic and lacks the phenolic OH, making F >= 20% plausible.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is galantamine, a small lipophilic tertiary-amine alkaloid with a methyl ether, no free phenol, no ketone, and favorable physicochemical properties. The high-transfer low-F records involve distinct liabilities absent in the query (tetrabenazine ketone metabolism, phenolic conjugation in ThH/M6G, poor solubility of terameprocol). Low-transfer records for basic alkaloids such as amisulpride and morphine show F >= 20%, supporting a high prediction.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement for galantamine in the provided evidence.",
    "No human pharmacokinetic data for the query molecule itself.",
    "First-pass metabolism and transporter effects for the query are not experimentally characterized.",
    "Structural similarity to low-F analogues is limited by the absence of phenol, ketone, and glucuronide liabilities."
  ],
